In /Users/akuppala/Documents/26aiRAG/spark-console/service/src/21-apps.mjs, replace appAnswers (lines 15-23) so that a port which took the connection and then closed it also counts as answering:

// Whether something answers at host:port: any HTTP reply counts (a login page, a redirect, an error page), and so does
// a service that isn't a web page (a database): it took the connection, then closed it or sent something that isn't
// HTTP. A port nothing listens on, or one that says nothing for `timeout`, doesn't. Never rejects, and keeps no body.
function appAnswers(host, port, timeout = 4000) {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    let connected = false;
    const req = http.get({ host, port, path: '/', timeout, agent: false }, res => { res.resume(); resolve(true); });
    req.on('socket', s => s.once('connect', () => { connected = true; }));
    req.on('timeout', () => { resolve(false); req.destroy(); });
    req.on('error', e => resolve(String(e.code || '').startsWith('HPE_') || (connected && e.code === 'ECONNRESET')));
  });
}

Notes on the change:
- resolve(false) now comes before req.destroy() in the timeout handler, so the "socket hang up" that destroy() raises can never turn a timeout into true.
- Nothing listening (ECONNREFUSED), unreachable, and silent-until-timeout all stay false. The existing "one that never answers is stopped" test uses PDEAD, a port nothing listens on, so it is unaffected.
- No change is needed in appUp, appPutBack or keep_running: appUp returns as soon as the database accepts a connection, and should_be_running stops reporting a healthy database as not answering.
- Over the tailnet from Spark 2, Docker forwards the published port straight to the container, so a connect succeeds only once the app listens. On a setup where Docker's userland proxy accepts first, a web app still booting could count as up a few seconds early.

